{"reason": "The current solution achieves high accuracy (99.44%) but exhibits overfitting—training loss drops to ~0.9 while validation loss plateaus around 0.875, and validation log-loss stalls near 0.071 after epoch 158. Root cause: the model uses only handcrafted tabular features (margin/shape/texture) and a single hidden fusion layer, limiting its ability to generalize. The available leaf images are completely unused, representing a rich source of discriminative information. Adding a pretrained CNN (ResNet18) to extract deep visual features from the binary leaf images would break the plateau, as historical successful experiments in memory show deep features improve performance without overfitting when combined with tabular inputs. Modifying training with mixup augmentation and progressive label smoothing (already partially using label smoothing) can further regularize and improve calibration under limited data. Therefore, I focus on data_processing_and_feature_engineering to integrate CNN features and model_design to incorporate a vision branch and adjust the fusion/regularization, with training_evaluation modifications to implement mixup and a refined training strategy.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Change #1: [Category: Data Augmentation / Feature Engineering] - What: Add a frozen pretrained ResNet18 feature extractor on the leaf images. Load images, resize to 224x224, normalize with ImageNet stats, and extract 512-dim features from the penultimate layer (the average pooling output) using a pretrained model. These features are appended as new columns to the existing tabular feature set. - Why: The current solution completely ignores the provided leaf images, which encode shape, margin, and texture information in raw pixel form. A pretrained CNN can capture high-level visual patterns that complement the handcrafted 64-dim histograms, providing a richer representation and breaking the validation loss plateau. - How: Use timm to load resnet18 (pretrained=True), set to eval mode, and extract features for all train/test images. Concatenate these 512 features with the engineered tabular features before scaling. Ensure split-first: extract features on the full dataset without using labels (unsupervised), then apply StandardScaler fit on training fold only. Preserve the original feature groups for the multi-branch model.", "model_design": "Change #2: [Category: Model Architecture] - What: Extend LeafClassificationModel to accept an additional input: the 512-dim image features. Add a dedicated vision encoder branch (a small MLP with LayerNorm, GELU, and dropout) that maps image features to the same hidden_dim (192) as the margin/shape/texture encoders. Incorporate this embedding into the self-attention fusion (i.e., four group embeddings instead of three) and adjust the fusion output and classifier input dimension accordingly (hidden_dim * 4 for concatenation). - Why: The overarching goal is to leverage visual information from the images. A separate vision branch allows the model to learn non-linear combinations of deep features alongside tabular features, while the self-attention fusion learns dynamic weighting across all four modalities. This architecture directly addresses the plateau by providing additional discriminative signal. - How: Modify LeafFeatureEncoder to be reused for vision features. Update SelfAttentionFusion to accept 4 embeddings. In forward(), accept an additional `image_features` argument, encode it, pass to fusion, and concatenate with the max-pooled embeddings and cross-correlation embedding. Keep the classifier width (hidden_dim) and label smoothing (0.1) unchanged. Ensure checkpoint compatibility by initializing fresh weights.", "training_evaluation": "Change #3: [Category: Regularization / Training Strategy] - What: Add mixup augmentation during training. For each batch, sample a mixup ratio λ from Beta(0.2, 0.2), mix the tabular features and image features (only for the tabular part since image features are already extracted) between pairs of samples, and mix the one-hot targets accordingly. Modify the loss computation to use the mixed targets with the cross-entropy loss (or binary cross-entropy on soft targets). Additionally, adjust the training schedule: use a longer cosine annealing with warmup (e.g., 5 epochs warmup, T_max=150) and set the early stopping patience to 25. - Why: With only 891 training samples, overfitting is a key issue. Mixup acts as a strong regularizer by creating virtual training examples, improving generalization and calibration. The current training already plateaus; mixup and a warmer schedule can help the model escape local minima and achieve lower validation log-loss. - How: In the training loop, for each batch, generate mixup indices and λ, compute mixed inputs and mixed targets (using label smoothing to avoid extreme probabilities), then compute loss as the average of per-sample cross-entropy on the mixed targets. Keep the validation protocol unchanged (no mixup). Update the optimizer (still AdamW with lr=3e-4) and scheduler (CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts or CosineAnnealingLR with warmup). Print epoch metrics as before."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current solution achieves high accuracy (99.44%) but exhibits overfitting—training loss drops to ~0.9 while validation loss plateaus around 0.875, and validation log-loss stalls near 0.071 after epoch 158. Root cause: the model uses only handcrafted tabular features (margin/shape/texture) and a single hidden fusion layer, limiting its ability to generalize. The available leaf images are completely unused, representing a rich source of discriminative information. Adding a pretrained CNN (ResNet18) to extract deep visual features from the binary leaf images would break the plateau, as historical successful experiments in memory show deep features improve performance without overfitting when combined with tabular inputs. Modifying training with mixup augmentation and progressive label smoothing (already partially using label smoothing) can further regularize and improve calibration under limited data. Therefore, I focus on data_processing_and_feature_engineering to integrate CNN features and model_design to incorporate a vision branch and adjust the fusion/regularization, with training_evaluation modifications to implement mixup and a refined training strategy.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
