tio λ from Beta(0.2, 0.2), mix the tabular features and image features (only for the tabular part since image features are already extracted) between pairs of samples, and mix the one-hot targets accordingly. Modify the loss computation to use the mixed targets with the cross-entropy loss (or binary cross-entropy on soft targets). Additionally, adjust the training schedule: use a longer cosine annealing with warmup (e.g., 5 epochs warmup, T_max=150) and set the early stopping patience to 25. - Why: With only 891 training samples, overfitting is a key issue. Mixup acts as a strong regularizer by creating virtual training examples, improving generalization and calibration. The current training already plateaus; mixup and a warmer schedule can help the model escape local minima and achieve lower validation log-loss. - How: In the training loop, for each batch, generate mixup indices and λ, compute mixed inputs and mixed targets (using label smoothing to avoid extreme probabilities), then compute loss as the average of per-sample cross-entropy on the mixed targets. Keep the validation protocol unchanged (no mixup). Update the optimizer (still AdamW with lr=3e-4) and scheduler (CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts or CosineAnnealingLR with warmup). Print epoch metrics as before."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
